The @, % and $ operators read RAM directly, so they missed memory that the
host keeps elsewhere and copies back only on demand, such as the screen.  A
peek hook lets the host bring that memory up to date first.  It runs only
while the host's dirty byte is set and the read overlaps the host's
0x2000-byte window, so other reads cost one or two extra loads and no call.
The operators also wrap addresses at 0x10000 as PICO-8 does, instead of
reading 0 past 0xffff.

